WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:10.250
SPEAKER: To get started with
the Speech API for Node.js,

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.670
you'll need a GCP project, which
you can set up in the console.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.500
Enable the Speech
API for that project,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.650
then, create a service account
and download the private key

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.478
as Jason.

00:00:26.478 --> 00:00:28.020
You're going to use
these credentials

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.880
to access Google Cloud APIs, so
keep this file secure and out

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.335
of your code and public repos.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.770
To access the credential
securely from your project,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.470
go to the terminal and set
an environment variable

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.030
to the path of your
service account.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.940
Now, prepare your environment
Node.js development.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.560
Install the client library.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.450
If you use Cloud
Shell on the console,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.300
you can skip this step, since
the required dependencies

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.790
are already included.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.310
For this example, you
will need some audio which

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.650
contains speech to transcribe.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.450
There's a sample
audio you can download

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.210
linked in the notes below.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.745
Move your audio sample to the
project in a resources folder,

00:01:15.745 --> 00:01:17.370
and change the name
of the file to make

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.520
a generic for example purposes.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.640
Now, you don't actually have to
change the name of your file,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.590
but just remember to
write it correctly

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.540
when prompted to write
the file name to the code.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:35.020
Make a new project file
or open an existing file.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.870
Open the Google Cloud client
library and the file system

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.470
module.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.340
Start with an async
function called main,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.250
then instantiate
a speech client.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.350
Provide the file name
of the audio file

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.730
you want to transcribe,
then use file system to read

00:01:54.730 --> 00:02:00.250
the local audio file and
convert it to Base64 encoding.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.220
Create a document called
audio with a field called

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:08.280
content, set to that
base64 string of the audio.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.740
Provide some details
about configuration

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.900
in an object called config.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.180
You must include the
type of encoding,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.430
the sample rate in
Hertz, and the language

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.150
of the speech in the audio, but
there are also optional fields.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.430
You can find out more about
those fields in the API

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.600
documentation link below.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.860
Instantiate an object
called request,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.120
which is made up of the
audio and config objects.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.810
Call recognize, passing
the request object.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.550
This returns a
promise that resolves

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.000
to contain the result at zero
or more sequential speech

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.640
recognition result messages.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.370
Each speech recognition
result has a property

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.570
called alternatives, which may
contain one or more recognition

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.640
hypotheses.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.040
An alternative has three
properties, transcript,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.760
confidence and words.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.310
Transcript is a string
representing the words

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.330
that the user spoke.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.230
Confidence is a number
between 0.0 and 1.0,

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.080
where a higher number
indicates an estimated greater

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.340
likelihood that the
recognized words are correct.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.580
Words is an array of
word info objects.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.200
I won't talk more
about word info,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.420
since it goes beyond the
scope of this quick start.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.000
But if you're curious,
check out the API reference

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.220
to find out more.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.950
To print out the transcription,
get the first alternative

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.940
from each result, then join
them in a single string.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.170
Log that transcription.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.450
Run the main function
and include a catch block

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.000
to handle any errors.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.400
Save your file and run the code.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.860
If everything
worked properly, you

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.230
should see the
transcription of the text.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.640
If not, take a look back at
the code for any syntax errors.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.690
Congratulations.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.800
You've just sent your
first request to the Speech

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.710
to Text API.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.660
To find out more about
the client libraries

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:06.210
or consult speech basics, check
out the Cloud documentation.

